# Security: secrets and env

This project must handle credentials safely. Treat this as an ongoing requirement, not a one-time fix.

## Secrets storage

- **Never** commit tokens, DB passwords, or API keys to git.
- Local dev: `environment.env` in project root (gitignored via `*.env`).
- Template for setup: `environment.env.example` — placeholders only, no real values.
- Runtime: read via `AppEnv.get()` / `LAKI_NAILS_BOT_TOKEN`, not hardcoded in Kotlin or `application.yaml`.
- Production/deploy: host env vars or a secrets manager — not a file in the repo.

## Forbidden

- Putting `telegram.botToken` or passwords in committed yaml/properties.
- Logging secret values or full Telegram API URLs (they embed the bot token).
- Running tests that write junk into the production DB without a separate test database.

## Logging

- Default log level: `INFO` (see `logback.xml`).
- Do not enable `TRACE` on `io.ktor.client` — it leaks bot token in request URLs.
- App debug: `DEBUG` on `kirillale.lakinais` only when needed.

## If a secret leaks

- Revoke/regenerate in BotFather and rotate DB password.
- Update local `environment.env` only; never commit the new value.

## When implementing features

- Prefer env/config for anything sensitive.
- Add new secret keys to `environment.env.example` with empty or placeholder values.
- Before finishing work, confirm no secrets in diff or logs.
